# AI Coding Contract

## Architecture

1. Services orchestrate business workflows and enforce business rules.
2. Repositories handle persistence only.
3. Calculations and decisions live in dedicated collaborators such as calculators, policies, resolvers, strategies and state machines.
4. Events handle genuine cross-cutting side effects.
5. Transactions wrap every multi-step write.
6. Infrastructure must not leak into services.

If something can change independently, it must not live inside the workflow service.

## Actions versus services

Actions are only for single-purpose operations, for example:

- export CSV;
- import a bulk file;
- bulk update status;
- sync one external resource;
- regenerate one artefact;
- execute one narrow command.

Business workflows, rules, eligibility decisions and state transitions belong in services. Do not create an action as a generic use-case layer in front of every service.

## Dependency injection

- Inject every dependency through the constructor.
- Do not use facades, globals or sessions in services.
- Do not make static model calls.
- The only allowed static infrastructure call in a service is `Database::transaction()`.
- Wrap external systems behind injected project-owned interfaces or gateways.

## Transactions

- A method performing two or more database writes must use `Database::transaction()`.
- Every write belonging to the workflow must occur inside the transaction.
- The transaction callback must return a value.
- Critical failures must throw and roll back.
- Do not write before or after the transaction boundary as part of the same operation.

## Events

- Use events only for real side effects.
- Do not create events for possible future use.
- Every event must have at least one listener that performs work.
- Services must not directly call other services merely to perform side effects.
- Unit tests verify that the event is emitted.
- Do not test listener execution through the framework in a service unit test.

## Enums

- Do not introduce magic strings for status, type or action values.
- Use PHP 8.1+ enums.
- Validate input using `Enum::from()`, explicit `Enum::tryFrom()` handling, or an equivalent enum-aware validator.

## Error handling

- Critical flows involving money, rewards, orders or access must throw and roll back on failure.
- Non-critical analytics or tracking failures may be caught and logged.
- Empty catch blocks are forbidden.
- Never silently continue after a critical failure.
- Do not expose sensitive provider or infrastructure errors to public clients.

## Service responsibilities

Services may validate business rules, coordinate repositories, emit events, control transactions and call dedicated collaborators.

Services must not format presentation data, build complex queries, access request/session globals, use facades, make static model calls, or contain independently changing calculation logic.

## Repositories

Repositories own queries and writes only. They must not orchestrate workflows, emit domain events, format responses or decide business policy.

Site-owned queries and writes must include explicit site scope.

## Code quality

- Split code into another class only when it has an independent reason to change, improves naming, or isolates failure.
- Private methods must add semantic clarity.
- Prefer correctness and clarity over brevity.
- Suggest relevant improvements, but do not invent dependencies, configuration or behaviour.
- When required context is missing, ask before writing code.

## Unit testing and Mockery

- Every service requires unit tests.
- All unit-test mocks use Mockery.
- Mock only real classes or interfaces: `Mockery::mock(ClassName::class)`.
- Do not mock `stdClass`, arrays or anonymous objects.
- Model mocks must be partial mocks using `makePartial()` where real model behaviour is required.
- Use partial mocks only when orchestration genuinely requires real behaviour.
- Static method mocking is forbidden.
- Refactor static dependencies behind injectable abstractions before testing.
- Every test must contain at least one meaningful assertion.
- Tests must verify return values, state/side effects, or interactions with dependencies.
- Tests must be isolated and deterministic.
- Do not make real Stripe, API, queue, mail or other external calls.
- Test transaction use, event emission and rollback behaviour for relevant service workflows.
- Do not test framework internals.

Static coupling that prevents proper mocking is a design flaw and must be refactored.